en rather than an incidental gap. [Data: Entities (2294)]\\n\\nThe direct relationship between “CYCLES OF CHEMOTHERAPY” and “DAYS OF REST” reinforces that cycles are defined by the alternation of treatment and rest. In operational terms, this creates a schedule template that repeatedly integrates dosing and recovery. [Data: Relationships (3968); Entities (2276, 2294)]\"},{\"summary\":\"High-grade serous tumor subtype is linked to a six-cycle recommendation even in stage 1 guidance\",\"explanation\":\"Beyond advanced-stage guidance, this community includes subtype-specific guidance: six cycles of chemotherapy are recommended for high-grade serous tumors, and this is explicitly noted as coming from stage 1 guidance. This indicates that, for this subtype, the intensity/duration of chemotherapy (six cycles) may be elevated or standardized even when the stage context is earlier. [Data: Relationships (3966); Entities (2292, 2276)]\\n\\nThe “HIGH-GRADE SEROUS TUMOR” entity reiterates that six cycles are recommended even in stage 1 settings for this subtype, highlighting that tumor biology/subtype can drive regimen decisions independently of (or in addition to) stage-based rules. [Data: Entities (2292); Relationships (3966)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community’s structure reflects two decision axes: disease extent (stage) and tumor type (subtype)\",\"explanation\":\"One axis in the community is stage-based: stages 2–4 are explicitly grouped as advanced stages for which post-surgery chemotherapy is recommended, with six cycles commonly given. This links disease extent to both treatment indication (chemo after surgery) and typical duration (six cycles). [Data: Entities (2290); Relationships (3943)]\\n\\nA second axis is subtype-based: high-grade serous tumors are associated with a recommendation for six cycles (noted in stage 1 guidance). This shows a parallel driver of treatment intensity based on tumor subtype. [Data: Entities (2292, 2276); Relationships (3966)]\\n\\nThese axes